# templ copies HTML comment text verbatim into the string literals it generates,
# so a CRLF working tree (what core.autocrlf=true gives on Windows, the default
# on GitHub's windows runners) makes `templ generate` emit \r\n *inside* those
# literals. That is a real content change git's CRLF normalization cannot undo:
# every Windows build left the tree dirty, and the second `make` invocation in
# the release workflow stamped the binaries -dirty. Pin LF everywhere so
# generation is reproducible across platforms.
* text=auto eol=lf
